{"author":{"id":"f4e4dbe251fbd7519eab696986bae317e93a786739d6cebe9c4c1e89dd44e465"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793681967,"nonce":"KBmbqZlF8suW67GwnsKtEgjrytA=","title":"A [Closed] class whose constructor or initialiser throws is accepted","message":"Found by the M4 code review (adversarial reviewer). Listed under \"Not addressed here\" in https://github.com/icosahedron/csharp-ansatz/pull/1. Parent epic: `54c6e06f383039f9758f1571999c683db1c244561561998cf9a52c41b84fa52f`. Belongs with M6: `9002a2c212f7349c24dda905b33ed163c03e32e96b5d052ea82bc35c00e36618`.\n\nThe closed-shape check (`src/Warrant.Extraction/TypeChecks.cs`, `ClosedClass`) checks only the *shape* of a closed class's static instances: that each is an initialised get-only auto-property or `static readonly` field created by `new`. It does not look at what runs. The private constructor body and the initialiser arguments are arbitrary code that nobody verifies.\n\nIf that code throws, the type initialiser fails, and every access to the class throws `TypeInitializationException`. The model still has the instances, so a theorem such as `Prove.That(!Same(Outcome.A, Outcome.B))` is proved, while on the CLR the claim throws instead of being true.\n\nThis is not a proof of a false value, but it is a proof about a program that does not run as modelled.\n\nFix options, once M6 has an exception model:\n\n- Reject a closed class whose constructor body or initialiser arguments are anything but simple assignments of constants or parameters.\n- Model the type initialiser, so that a throw is part of the model.\n\nDone when a rejected-corpus member shows a closed class with a throwing constructor, and it is either rejected or its theorems reflect the throw.","files":null}]}